window.SPL_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"Build AI workflows in minutes, not hours","page":"Introduction","href":"introduction.html","text":"A .spl file is TypeScript with instructions for models and agents written between the lines. One file holds the code, the prompts, the types that check model output, the guards and the human approval.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Three additions to TypeScript","page":"Introduction","href":"introduction.html#three-additions-to-typescript","text":"Everything in a .spl file is TypeScript except three things. Syntax Meaning --- --- //* ... *// A [Markdown comment](Language.md#markdown-comments): prose for readers and for spl doc --- ... --- A [block](Language.md#blocks): an optional header of key: value lines, then an instruction for a model @name , ->[Type] @name Inside a block: a [reference](LLM-commands.md#references) to a variable, and an output the block declares A block has one of three modes. An ask block is one prompt and one model call. An agent block lets the model act with tools until it has saved every output. A codegen block generates functions.","group":"Start here","signatures":["//*","*//","---","---","@name","->[Type] @name"]},{"title":"Generate reusable functions","page":"Introduction","href":"introduction.html#generate-reusable-functions","text":"A codegen block asks the model for JavaScript functions and declares them as typed constants. Later code calls them like any other function. In code, await codegen(description) does the same and returns the function. The generated code is saved beside the workflow, here as codegen.sieve.js. Its first line hashes the description, and later runs reuse a matching file without calling the model: See Codegen blocks for the checks and the cache rules.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Types the model can read","page":"Introduction","href":"introduction.html#types-the-model-can-read","text":"Types are ordinary values built with library functions. They check data, convert what a model sends into JavaScript objects and render the JSON Schema that the model receives, including any descriptions you attach. Union(Float, Int) accepts either number. .methods({...}) makes converted answers objects with methods, so person.toString() works on the model's output. outputType: Person gives the block's output the TypeScript type of Person. See Types.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Why SPL?","page":"Introduction","href":"introduction.html#why-spl","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"How a run works","page":"Introduction","href":"introduction.html#how-a-run-works","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Non-goals","page":"Introduction","href":"introduction.html#non-goals","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Where next","page":"Introduction","href":"introduction.html#where-next","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Getting started","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html","text":"Install the CLI, configure OpenAI, Anthropic or Google credentials, then write and refine a workflow in a short edit-run loop.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Install","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#install","text":"Use Node.js >=24.15.0. The terminal view is pure JavaScript (Ink), so it runs wherever Node.js does. If it cannot start, the CLI reports the problem and keeps complete plain logs. spl login \u003cprovider> prompts for an API key without echoing it and saves a separate key for each provider in ~/.spl/credentials.json (override the folder with SPL_HOME). It also selects that provider as the default. If the provider's environment key is already set, login selects the provider without prompting or copying the environment secret into the file. Keys are configured locally; login does not make a billable request or verify account access. Alternatively, set OPENAI_API_KEY, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY or GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY. Google also accepts GOOGLE_API_KEY and GEMINI_API_KEY, in that order after the canonical name. Environment keys take precedence over saved keys. A single configured provider is detected automatically. With multiple providers, select one with --provider, SPL_PROVIDER, a model such as --model google/gemini-2.5-flash, or a saved login default. See Models and authentication for precedence and defaults. Workflows without model calls, spl check, spl compile, spl doc and replay need no credentials.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Write a workflow","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#write-a-workflow","text":"The code is TypeScript and runs top to bottom. The --- block is an instruction for a model: @name passes the variable in, and outputVar: greeting names the constant that receives the answer. success ends the run with a result, which the CLI prints on stdout.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"File structure","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#file-structure","text":"Statements and blocks run in file order. success(value) or an uncaught failure ends the run.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"TypeScript in five rules","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#typescript-in-five-rules","text":"Workflow code is ordinary TypeScript. These rules cover what is specific to workflows; Workflow code explains each one.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"The iteration loop","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#the-iteration-loop","text":"Replay needs the same model requests in the same order. After changing the file, record a new trace. Recorded model calls, tools and human answers are not repeated during replay; your own code runs again. See Traces and replay.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Use the library without a .spl file","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#use-the-library-without-a-spl-file","text":"The same functions are a normal Node.js package. Install spl-lang in a project and import what you need; see The library in Node.js programs. A workflow can also become a module: spl compile writes a TypeScript file whose default export runs the workflow, and one .spl file can import another.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"VS Code extension","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#vs-code-extension","text":"Install from Marketplace: SPL The extension in vscode/ gives .spl files a TypeScript editing experience. See the extension guide for details. To install from the repository, build the package and install the .vsix file it writes:","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Run the tests","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#run-the-tests","text":"Tests are offline and never contact a model.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Workflow code","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html","text":"A .spl file is TypeScript running on Node.js, with the spl-lang library already in scope, Markdown comments and --- blocks. This page covers the file format, the TypeScript dialect, imports, the library, types, the Markdown builder, inputs, logging, tools and errors.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"The file format","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#the-file-format","text":"A .spl file is TypeScript with three extensions: Markdown comments, blocks, and references inside blocks.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Markdown comments","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#markdown-comments","text":"A Markdown comment starts with a line that begins with //* and ends with a line that contains only *//. Leading whitespace is allowed before both markers.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Code","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#code","text":"Everything outside Markdown comments and blocks is TypeScript. The scanner skips strings, template literals, regular expressions and comments, so --- or //* inside them is never treated as syntax.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Blocks","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#blocks","text":"A line containing only --- opens a block, and the next such line closes it. Whitespace around the dashes is allowed. A block is an optional header, then the instruction. The mode key selects what the block does: Mode The block is Outputs --- --- --- ask (default) One prompt and one model call, without tools One, named by outputVar agent One agent call: the model acts with tools Any number, written ->[Type] @name in the instruction codegen One model call that writes functions Functions, written -> @name(params): Type Natural language & models describes each mode.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["ask","agent","codegen"]},{"title":"File defaults","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#file-defaults","text":"A block that has header lines but no instruction sets defaults for the whole file.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Placement and scope","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#placement-and-scope","text":"A block is a statement. It may stand wherever a statement may: at the top level, in function bodies, loops, if and try.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"How a file is compiled","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#how-a-file-is-compiled","text":"The front scanner turns a .spl file into TypeScript. Code is copied as written, a Markdown comment becomes blank lines, and each block becomes a call to the library on the lines it occupied. Every construct stays on its original line, so diagnostics and stack traces point at .spl lines without source maps. The agent block of basics.spl becomes roughly this, on lines 25 to 33: The joke block becomes:","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"The TypeScript dialect","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#the-typescript-dialect","text":"Workflow code is TypeScript that Node.js can run by erasing the types. Syntax Error --- --- enum Color { ... } Unsupported TypeScript syntax: enum cannot be erased; use a union of string literals instead of enum namespace Name { ... } Unsupported TypeScript syntax: namespace cannot be erased; use an ES module or a plain object instead of namespace @decorator Unsupported TypeScript syntax: decorators cannot be erased; call the decorator as a function instead constructor(private x: number) Unsupported TypeScript syntax: parameter properties cannot be erased; declare the field and assign it in the constructor","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["enum Color { ... }","namespace Name { ... }","@decorator","constructor(private x: number)"]},{"title":"Await model calls and tools","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#await-model-calls-and-tools","text":"ask, agent, decision, human, codegen, restrict and the tools return promises. Use await whenever you need the result or the effect to have happened. A call without await keeps running in the background: its result may arrive after the workflow has ended, and its failure cannot be caught by the workflow and crashes the process. With --trace, recorded calls run one at a time in call order, so Promise.all stays replayable. Code runs as ordinary JavaScript on the main thread. A long synchronous loop blocks log rendering, timers and Ctrl+C handling until it yields; give long loops an await point, or move heavy work to a worker thread or child process.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Imports and modules","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#imports-and-modules","text":"Imports work as in any ES module: a relative specifier resolves from the file that contains the import, not from the working directory. Import Resolves to --- --- node:fs , node:path , node:child_process , ... The whole Node.js standard library ./helper.js , ../lib/util.mjs Your own modules, relative to the importing file ./helper.ts TypeScript modules with erasable types ./other.spl Another workflow, compiled when it is imported ./data.json with with { type: 'json' } JSON modules lodash , @scope/pkg Packages in node_modules , found from the importing file upward await import(specifier) Any of the above, loaded on demand A helper module is ordinary JavaScript or TypeScript. It imports the library explicitly, and its calls take part in the running workflow: same model, restrictions, guidance, logs and trace. spl-lang and spl-lang/markdown always resolve to the running copy of the package, so helpers need no local install and share the workflow's runtime and Failure class. Modules are loaded natively by Node.js: they are cached for the lifetime of the process and their source is not recorded in traces, so a replay imports them from disk again.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["node:fs","node:path","node:child_process","./helper.js","../lib/util.mjs","./helper.ts","./other.spl","./data.json","with { type: 'json' }","lodash","@scope/pkg","await import(specifier)"]},{"title":"What a .spl module exports","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#what-a-spl-module-exports","text":"A .spl file is a module. Its default export is its workflow, and its exported declarations are its named exports. spl check follows .spl imports and checks those files too, and spl compile writes a module for each of them.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"The library","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#the-library","text":"A .spl file can use these names without importing them. Other modules import them from spl-lang. The API reference lists every signature and option. Group Names --- --- Model calls ask agent decision People human Guidance and restrictions enforce forbid restrict Control success fail assert Failure Exact tools readFile writeFile webFetch webSearch shell Code generation codegen Inputs and logging args env log Types Str Int Float Bool Char Nil Any Dict Fn List Union Nullable Enum Struct Typedef JsonSchema validate t Markdown markdown Model calls and guidance are described in Natural language & models; restrictions, failures and human input in Guards & failures.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":[]},{"title":"Types","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#types","text":"A type is a value that describes data. It checks values, converts what a model sends into JavaScript objects and renders the JSON Schema a model receives. Type names start with an uppercase letter. Use one as outputType in an ask block, in ->[Type] @name in an agent block, as schema in ask(), or directly. Builder Accepts --- --- Str Strings Int Safe integers ( Number.isSafeInteger ) Float Finite numbers, including integers Bool true or false Char Strings of exactly one Unicode character Nil null Any Any JSON value Dict Any plain object, without a declared shape Fn Functions; cannot be described as JSON List(T) Arrays whose items satisfy T Union(A, B, ...) Values satisfying any member Nullable(T) T or null ; a nullable field must still be present Enum('a', 'b') One of the listed strings Struct(name, fields) Exact records: every field required, extra fields rejected Typedef(name, type, annotations?) The same values as type , under a name JsonSchema(schema, name?) Values valid against raw JSON Schema, constraints enforced Every type has the same methods: Method Result --- --- Type.parse(value) The validated value, or a validation_failed failure such as $.lines[0].quantity: expected Int, got 1.5 Type.coerce(value) The value converted to the type, or the same failure Type.parseJson(text) Like parse , from strict JSON text; malformed text is invalid_format Type.is(value) true or false , without throwing Type.schema() JSON Schema, with annotations in description validate(value, Type) Same as Type.parse(value) ; with raw JSON Schema it checks and returns the value Types are immutable: annotate, describe, as, nullable(), list() and or(other) return new types. Struct('Point').field('x', Float, { desc: 'Horizontal' }).field('y', Float) is the fluent form of a struct. t holds every builder under one name, for code that prefers t.Str and t.List(t.Int).","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["Str","Int","Float","Bool","Char","Nil","Any","Dict","Fn","List(T)","Union(A, B, ...)","Nullable(T)","Enum('a', 'b')","Struct(name, fields)","Typedef(name, type, annotations?)","JsonSchema(schema, name?)","Type.parse(value)","Type.coerce(value)","Type.parseJson(text)","Type.is(value)","Type.schema()","validate(value, Type)"]},{"title":"Parse and coerce","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#parse-and-coerce","text":"parse validates. It never converts, fills defaults or drops fields: Int.parse('1') fails, and a missing nullable field is an error. It returns a copy for structs and lists. coerce converts a value to the type first, then validates it. It is deterministic and never calls a model. Target coerce accepts in addition --- --- Any type A string that holds JSON, where the type expects an object, a list or any value: it is parsed first Int , Float Numeric strings. Int still requires an exact integer: '3.0' gives 3 , but 3.7 fails Bool 1 and 0 , and the strings 'true' , 'false' , '1' and '0' Str Any other JSON value, turned into JSON text: Str.coerce(42) is '42' Nil The string 'null' Struct , List Fields and items are coerced recursively Union A value that already fits a member is kept as it is; conversion is the fallback Enum and Char values are not converted. A value that still does not fit throws validation_failed with the path of the bad field: The runtime calls coerce wherever a model produces a typed value: the outputType of an ask block, the schema of ask() and every output an agent saves. Asking the model to try again is separate: see repairs. Error paths start at $. When an agent saves an output, the output's name is the root instead: invoice.tax: expected Money, got 3312.5. A type named with Typedef appears under its own name, as Money does here.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["Int","Float","Bool","Str","Nil","Struct","List","Union"]},{"title":"Annotations are advice","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#annotations-are-advice","text":".annotate({...}), .describe(text) and the third argument of Typedef attach arbitrary metadata for the model. desc or description becomes the schema description; every other key is appended as Model advice (not validation constraints): {\"minimum\":0}. Annotations never reject a value: Money.parse(-1) succeeds. Check business rules with assert, or use JsonSchema({ type: 'integer', minimum: 0 }) when a constraint must be enforced.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Classes","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#classes","text":".methods({...}) gives parsed objects methods; .class(Constructor) makes them instances of an existing class, using its static fromJSON(data) when present. For a class type, is requires an instance, parse and coerce accept plain data or an existing instance, and Person.data is the plain-struct view with the same fields. Instances serialize like plain objects with JSON.stringify.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"TypeScript types","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#typescript-types","text":"Infer gives the TypeScript type a builder stands for. It is a global in .spl files; other modules import it from spl-lang. Blocks and model calls are typed by their types, so invoice below is known to have a numeric total.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Markdown builder","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#markdown-builder","text":"markdown builds Markdown text without string concatenation. Every method returns the document, so calls chain; toString() returns the result. Method Output --- --- heading(text, level = 1) Heading with level 1 through 6 paragraph(text) Paragraph containing inline Markdown bulletList(items) Bullet list of strings; multiline items are indented orderedList(items, start = 1) Numbered list quote(text) Blockquote, including multiline text codeBlock(text, language = '') Fenced code block; the fence grows to fit backticks in the text table(headers, rows) GFM table; rows must match the header width thematicBreak() Horizontal rule raw(text) Markdown as written toString() Blocks joined with blank lines and a final newline; '' for an empty document Inline helpers return strings: markdown.bold(text), italic(text), code(text), link(label, url), image(alt, url) and escape(text). Block methods accept Markdown, so use escape for literal dynamic text. Link labels and image alt text are escaped automatically, and table cells escape |. Invalid input, such as a heading level of 7 or a multiline heading, raises assertion_failed. Other modules import the builder from spl-lang/markdown; new markdown.Document() is equivalent to markdown.document().","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["heading(text, level = 1)","paragraph(text)","bulletList(items)","orderedList(items, start = 1)","quote(text)","codeBlock(text, language = '')","table(headers, rows)","thematicBreak()","raw(text)","toString()"]},{"title":"Inputs: args and env","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#inputs-args-and-env","text":"args is the read-only list of --arg values. env is the read-only process environment. Missing entries are undefined. With --trace, the first use of env records the environment so that replay sees the same values; process.env is not recorded. In a block, @args and @env.NAME are references to the same values.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Logging","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#logging","text":"log(...values) writes to the run log on stderr at info level; log.error(...) and log.debug(...) select other levels. Debug output appears only with --log-level debug, and --silent hides all logs. Objects and arrays are logged as indented JSON: See Output and exit codes.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Exact tools","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#exact-tools","text":"Tools are ordinary async functions that never call a model. Paths are relative to the working directory, and every call is checked against active restrictions. Function Result --- --- await readFile(path) File contents as UTF-8 text await writeFile(path, content) Writes the file, creating parent folders, and returns a confirmation await webFetch(url) Response body as text (30 s timeout, up to 5 redirects) await webSearch(query) Search results as text await shell(command) Combined stdout/stderr; a nonzero exit raises tool_error (120 s timeout) shell runs through the platform shell (cmd.exe on Windows, /bin/sh elsewhere). Agents see the same tools under the same names.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["await readFile(path)","await writeFile(path, content)","await webFetch(url)","await webSearch(query)","await shell(command)"]},{"title":"Errors","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#errors","text":"Library failures are Failure objects: error.statusCode (also error.status_code) and error.reason say what happened. Catch them with try/catch and rethrow anything else. Any other uncaught exception, such as a TypeError in your code or an error thrown by an imported module, ends the workflow as a runtime_error whose reason is Name: message and whose line is the line that threw. success(value) ends the run by throwing a signal that is not an Error; a catch-all must rethrow it. See Guards & failures.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"What the runtime does not see","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#what-the-runtime-does-not-see","text":"The runtime records, replays and restricts library calls. Code that reaches the outside world directly is your own responsibility: Library ( readFile , shell , webFetch , agent , blocks, MCP) Node.js APIs ( node:fs , fetch , child_process ) --- --- --- Checked by restrict Yes No Recorded by --trace Yes No On replay Recorded result is returned Runs again Values from Date.now(), Math.random() or a direct file read can differ between a recording and its replay. If such a value reaches a prompt or a tool argument, the replayed request no longer matches the recording and the run stops with a mismatch. Use the library tools for anything a replay must reproduce. Imported modules are in the same position: they are loaded from disk again on every replay, so a trace of a workflow that imports local files depends on those files staying as they were.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["restrict","--trace"]},{"title":"The library in Node.js programs","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#the-library-in-nodejs-programs","text":"Outside a .spl file, the library is a regular package. Calls use a default runtime: provider keys from the environment or spl login, info-level logs on stderr (override with SPL_LOG_LEVEL), the process working directory and stdin for human. configure(options) is optional and replaces the default runtime. Options: model, provider, cwd, args, logLevel (silent, error, info, debug), logger, backend, timeoutMs, mcpServers, trace and human. Running whole workflows from a program is described under Embedding.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Natural language & models","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html","text":"A --- block is an instruction for a model, written in plain language between lines of code; ask and agent call the model from code, and decision turns a question into a boolean. This page covers the three block modes, references and outputs, models and reasoning, decisions, and how to check what comes back.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Blocks","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#blocks","text":"A block holds a header and an instruction; Workflow code describes the syntax. Its mode decides how the instruction is carried out. Mode What runs Tools Outputs --- --- --- --- ask (default) One prompt, one model call None One: outputVar , converted with outputType agent One agent call that acts until its outputs are saved Variables, functions, files, web, shell, MCP Any number: ->[Type] @name codegen One model call that writes JavaScript functions None Functions: -> @name(params): Type Both blocks are ask blocks. The first converts the answer to a boolean, so isFriday has the TypeScript type boolean. The second has no outputType, so complaint is the reply as text.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["ask","agent","codegen"]},{"title":"The header","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#the-header","text":"The header is the run of key: value lines at the start of a block. Values are YAML, except outputType, which is a TypeScript expression. Key Modes Value Meaning --- --- --- --- mode all ask , agent or codegen What the block does; ask when omitted model all Model id Model for this block; provider/model selects a provider reasoning all none , low , medium , high or max [Reasoning level](#models-and-reasoning); medium by default enforce all String or list of strings [Guidance](#guidance-enforce-and-forbid) the model must follow forbid all String or list of strings Guidance on what the model must avoid hide all true or false Hide reasoning in logs; responses stay visible tokens all Positive integer Maximum generated tokens per model turn restrict agent A folder, a list of folders, or a map with fs , exec , net , mcp [Restrictions](Safety.md#restrictions) for this block outputVar ask A variable name, or =name The output; required in ask blocks outputType ask Type expression The type the answer is converted to; Str by default repairs ask Integer 0 to 5 Retries when the answer cannot be converted to outputType seed ask Integer 0 to 2147483647 [Sampling](#sampling) seed temperature ask Number 0 to 2 Sampling temperature topP ask Number 0 to 1 Nucleus sampling A key used in a mode it does not apply to is an error, for example `outputVar` applies only to ask blocks or `restrict` applies only to agent blocks. model, reasoning, enforce, forbid, hide and tokens can also be file defaults. A list value continues on indented lines:","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["mode","model","reasoning","enforce","forbid","hide","tokens","restrict","outputVar","outputType","repairs","seed","temperature","topP"]},{"title":"References","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#references","text":"@name in an instruction refers to a variable, a function or a type of the surrounding code. Written Meaning --- --- @notes The variable, function or type notes @invoice.total A property: an identifier followed by .identifier parts @env.NAME The environment variable NAME @args The script arguments @@ A literal @ user@example.com Literal text: a reference starts a word For this block the model receives the instruction followed by the values: Referenced value Inlined in an ask block as --- --- Data Its JSON A function Its signature, such as \"function fib(n)\" A type Its JSON Schema undefined , such as a missing environment variable null","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["@notes","@invoice.total","@env.NAME","@args","@@","user@example.com","undefined"]},{"title":"Ask blocks","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#ask-blocks","text":"An ask block is one prompt and one model call. It has no tools: the model cannot read files, browse or run commands. The block above comes from extract-invoice.spl.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Agent blocks","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#agent-blocks","text":"An agent block is one agent call over the whole block. The model works through the instruction with tools: it reads the inputs, calls functions, acts on files, the web and the shell, and saves each output. Outputs are declared inline, where the instruction talks about them: Written Declares --- --- ->[Int] @count A constant count of type Int -> @notes An untyped output: any JSON value, typed unknown in TypeScript ->[List(Union(Str, Int))] @tags A type expression may contain balanced brackets -> [Str] @name Spaces between -> , [Type] and @name are optional ->[Int] @=count An assignment to the existing variable count","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["->[Int] @count","-> @notes","->[List(Union(Str, Int))] @tags","-> [Str]  @name","->[Int] @=count"]},{"title":"Agent tools","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#agent-tools","text":"Tool Purpose --- --- loadVariable(name) Read an input of the block. A type returns its complete JSON Schema saveVariable(name, value) Save an output as a JSON value that fits its declared type callFunction(name, args) Call an input that is a function, with positional JSON arguments, and return its result getEnv(name) Read one environment variable; a missing variable is null askHuman(question) Ask a person and return the answer setGuidance(kind, text) Add enforce or forbid guidance to the model calls made by functions called later in the block readFile , writeFile , webFetch , webSearch , shell The [exact tools](Language.md#exact-tools), within the active restrictions mcp0Search , ... [MCP tools](CLI.md#mcp-servers), named mcp\u003cIndex>\u003cTool>","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["loadVariable(name)","saveVariable(name, value)","callFunction(name, args)","getEnv(name)","askHuman(question)","setGuidance(kind, text)","readFile","writeFile","webFetch","webSearch","shell","mcp0Search"]},{"title":"Outputs and corrections","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#outputs-and-corrections","text":"A saved value is passed to Type.coerce of the output's type. An agent asks a person with askHuman when the instruction says so, as in color-poem.spl. See Human in the loop.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Codegen blocks","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#codegen-blocks","text":"A codegen block asks the model for JavaScript functions and declares them as constants. Generated code is cached beside the workflow: In code, await codegen(description, options?) generates from a description and resolves to its first function. Options are model, reasoning, hide and tokens, and a type argument gives the function its type, as codegen.spl shows.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"New or updated variables","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#new-or-updated-variables","text":"An output declares a new const in the enclosing scope. To assign an existing let instead, put = before the name: outputVar: =result in an ask block, ->[Int] @=count in an agent block.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Calls in code: ask and agent","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#calls-in-code-ask-and-agent","text":"await ask(prompt) returns the model's answer and await agent(prompt) lets the model act first. Use them where a prompt is built by code: in a loop, in a helper function, in a module. Model responses stream into the terminal view while the model is working, including available reasoning. hide: true suppresses reasoning, not responses. These are previews: JSON parsing, conversion and repairs use the completed response, never partial chunks. Traces keep complete responses rather than streaming chunks.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Options","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#options","text":"ask and agent take an options object as the second argument. Values are validated before the call; an unknown name or an invalid value is a runtime_error and no model is contacted. Option Values Effect --- --- --- len 'word' 'sentence' 'paragraph' 'long' Length hint (guidance only) fmt see [Output formats](#output-formats) Parse and check the answer schema A [type](Language.md#types) or JSON Schema draft-07 Convert or validate the answer; implies JSON repairs 0 to 5 Extra attempts after a format or validation error; not for agent reasoning 'none' 'low' 'medium' 'high' 'max' [Reasoning level](#models-and-reasoning) hide true or false (default) Suppress reasoning display when true ; responses remain visible model Model id Model for this call tokens Positive safe integer Maximum generated tokens per model turn, including reasoning seed Integer 0..2147483647 ask only: request repeatable [sampling](#sampling) where supported temperature Number 0..2 ask only: control sampling variability topP Number 0..1 ask only: control nucleus sampling tokens is a hard generation budget, not a length hint or a separate thinking budget. The same limit applies independently to every repair attempt and every agent turn. Omitting it keeps provider defaults. It maps to OpenAI max_output_tokens (or max_completion_tokens for seeded calls), Anthropic max_tokens, and Google maxOutputTokens. On Anthropic models that think with a fixed budget, tokens must exceed that budget; conflicting settings fail rather than silently enlarging the budget. If the model hits its token limit, the call fails with llm_error, not a successful partial answer, and repairs do not retry the truncated turn. Explicit budgets are part of the recorded request, so changing a budget cannot silently reuse an old answer during replay.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["len","fmt","schema","repairs","reasoning","hide","model","tokens","seed","temperature","topP"]},{"title":"Models and reasoning","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#models-and-reasoning","text":"The model of a call is chosen in this order: OpenAI, Anthropic and Google are supported through their official clients; use bare model ids or provider/model. See Models and authentication. reasoning sets how much the model thinks before it answers. Level Meaning --- --- none No reasoning, or the least the model allows low , medium , high Increasing effort max The strongest effort the model offers Provider low to high max none --- --- --- --- OpenAI Reasoning effort The highest effort the model has No reasoning, or the lowest effort of models that cannot turn it off Anthropic Adaptive thinking effort; on older models a thinking budget of 1024, 4096 or 16384 tokens The max effort, or a 32768-token budget Thinking disabled, or the least thinking the model allows Google Thinking level; on Gemini 2.5 the same budgets as Anthropic, capped by the model high , or the model's largest budget A zero budget or the minimum level; Pro models cannot disable thinking The effective provider, model and reasoning effort of each call are logged at info level and kept in traces.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["none","low","medium","high","max"]},{"title":"Sampling","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#sampling","text":"seed, temperature and topP limit randomness. They are header keys of ask blocks and options of ask(); agents and codegen do not accept them. Omitted sampling settings use provider and model defaults; no seed, temperature or topP is invented. The same values are reused for every repair attempt. Provider notes: The info log of each call shows the settings the provider adapter kept; null means the setting was omitted or unsupported and provider defaults apply. Traces include the requested sampling settings in the request identity. Even extreme settings cannot guarantee identical outputs from hosted models. Use --trace for deterministic replay.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Output formats","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#output-formats","text":"fmt is an option of ask() and agent() for answers that need no type. fmt Accepts JavaScript result --- --- --- 'text' (default) Trimmed text, no parsing string 'integer' JSON number without a fraction number 'float' Any finite JSON number number 'boolean' JSON true or false boolean 'array' JSON array Array 'object' JSON object (not an array) plain object 'json' Any JSON value any JSON value One enclosing code fence is removed; other prose around the JSON is rejected. A malformed or wrong-typed answer raises invalid_format.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["'text'","'integer'","'float'","'boolean'","'array'","'object'","'json'"]},{"title":"Types, schemas and repairs","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#types-schemas-and-repairs","text":"schema in ask() is what outputType is in an ask block. It accepts a type or raw JSON Schema. repairs: n, as a header key or an option, allows up to n more attempts. Each sends the original prompt again, followed by the previous answer and the validation error.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Guidance: enforce and forbid","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#guidance-enforce-and-forbid","text":"Guidance adds instructions to model calls. It shapes prompts; it does not check answers. Where Applies to --- --- enforce: and forbid: in a block header That block, and the model calls of functions its agent calls enforce: and forbid: in the file defaults Every model call of the file enforce(text, body) and forbid(text, body) in code Every model call made while body runs, including blocks inside it The agent tool setGuidance Model calls made by functions the agent calls afterwards enforce(text, body) and forbid(text, body) return what body returns, and the guidance follows the call across await and into the functions it invokes. text is a string or a list of strings. Guidance from all four places combines.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["enforce:","forbid:","enforce:","forbid:","enforce(text, body)","forbid(text, body)","setGuidance"]},{"title":"Decisions","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#decisions","text":"await decision({ question, evidence, ... }) answers a question about the evidence with a boolean, so code can branch on it without parsing an answer. It has two forms: Option Values Effect --- --- --- question Nonempty string What to decide; build it with a template literal evidence A string or a list What the question is judged against; objects in the list are shown as JSON cutoff Number 0..1 , default 0.5 Yes/no form: the probability that must be exceeded labels { label: boolean, ... } Label form: the labels to choose from and what each stands for model Model id Selects the provider, like model of ask Providers. OpenAI and Google answer with their decision endpoints, which use a fixed model whatever model the workflow names: gpt-6-luna on OpenAI's /v1/decisions and gemini-decision-1 on Google's /v1beta/decisions. The model of the call, the file default or --model only selects the provider. Anthropic has no decision endpoint, so on Anthropic models the runtime simulates one with an ask call of the selected model that returns probabilities as JSON, checked against a JSON Schema with one repair. The probability, or the chosen label, is written to the run log. Guidance from enforce and forbid applies to decisions too. Decisions are recorded in traces and replay without contacting the provider. An invalid option is a runtime_error before any call; a refusal or an unexpected answer is an llm_error.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["question","evidence","cutoff","labels","model"]},{"title":"Guards & failures","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html","text":"Model output varies, so SPL makes checks explicit: restrict limits actions, types and assert check data, failures can be caught, and human hands decisions to a person.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Restrictions","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#restrictions","text":"await restrict(spec, body) limits what tools and agents may touch while body runs, and resolves to what body returns. Outside any restriction, tools and agents are allowed by default. A blocked action raises restriction_violation. Spec Allows --- --- restrict('./out', body) or restrict({ fs: './out' }, body) Files inside the folder only restrict({ net: 'api.example.com' }, body) Only these hosts ( *.example.com matches subdomains) restrict({ exec: 'npm,git' }, body) Only these executables restrict({ mcp: 'docs/search' }, body) Only these MCP servers ( docs , docs/* ) or tools ( docs/search ) Each value is a comma-separated string or a list of strings. One object can combine several kinds. Nested restrictions of the same kind intersect, so a scope can only narrow. The restriction follows the call: it applies to everything body does, including blocks inside it, functions it calls and code after an await, and ends when body settles. An agent block sets its own restrictions with the restrict header key. The value is a folder, a list of folders, or a map with the same four kinds: The runtime applies them before the agent starts, to its tools, to the functions it calls and to MCP tools, and drops them when the block ends. They intersect with restrictions that are already active. restrict is a key of agent blocks only: ask and codegen blocks have no tools. What is checked: Models are told the active restrictions, and the runtime enforces them on every call. Restrictions govern the library, not Node.js. readFile, writeFile, webFetch, webSearch, shell, agents and MCP tools are checked. Code that calls node:fs, fetch or child_process directly is not: inside restrict('./out', ...), fs.writeFileSync('/etc/x') still runs. Use the library tools wherever a restriction should apply.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["restrict('./out', body)","restrict({ fs: './out' }, body)","restrict({ net: 'api.example.com' }, body)","restrict({ exec: 'npm,git' }, body)","restrict({ mcp: 'docs/search' }, body)"]},{"title":"Assertions, fail and success","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#assertions-fail-and-success","text":"All three work by throwing, so the statements after fail or success do not run. In a workflow that another file imported and called, success ends that call and fail rejects it.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Failure payload","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#failure-payload","text":"Every failure has the same shape. Uncaught failures are printed to stdout and exit with code 1. Field Value --- --- status Always failure reason What went wrong line The line of the .spl file that caused the failure command That line, trimmed status_code One of the codes below source The path of the file the failure is in. Present only when it is not the workflow that was run: an imported .spl file or generated code status_code Cause --- --- invalid_format An answer is not valid JSON, or not the requested fmt ; generated code is not function declarations validation_failed A value does not match its type or schema; an agent did not produce an output assertion_failed An assert was false; invalid input to the Markdown builder explicit_failure fail was called restriction_violation An action was blocked by restrict tool_error Missing file, HTTP failure, nonzero shell exit, or an MCP tool error llm_error Authentication, timeout, transport, token limit or model access problem; reasoning combined with sampling that the provider rejects runtime_error An uncaught JavaScript exception, an invalid option or argument, a missing human handler, an agent reaching for a name its block does not mention Syntax errors are different: they are found before anything runs, cannot be caught, and exit with code 2. Type errors are reported by spl check only; spl run does not look for them.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["status","reason","line","command","status_code","source","invalid_format","validation_failed","assertion_failed","explicit_failure","restriction_violation","tool_error","llm_error","runtime_error"]},{"title":"try / catch","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#try--catch","text":"Failures are Failure objects and can be caught like any exception. Property Value --- --- error.statusCode , error.status_code One of the codes above error.reason , error.message The explanation error.payload() The JSON payload error.line , error.command The workflow line that raised the failure and its source text; 0 and '' when the line is unknown Three rules keep error handling safe: A block is a statement, so it can stand inside try like any call:","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["error.statusCode","error.status_code","error.reason","error.message","error.payload()","error.line","error.command"]},{"title":"Checked model output","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#checked-model-output","text":"Each layer checks something different. Use the first that fits and add code for the rest. Layer What it does When it fails --- --- --- outputType , ->[Type] @name , schema Converts the answer with [ Type.coerce ](Language.md#parse-and-coerce) and checks its shape validation_failed with the path of the bad field repairs in ask blocks and ask() Sends the answer and the error back to the model, up to 5 times The last error is raised Corrections in agent blocks Returns an invalid save to the agent, up to 2 times per output validation_failed assert in code Checks business rules: totals, ranges, cross-references assertion_failed enforce , forbid , annotations Advise the model; they check nothing Never Agents get no repairs: a failed check never repeats their actions. A tool error stops an agent block at once instead of being retried.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["outputType","->[Type] @name","schema","repairs","ask()","assert","enforce","forbid"]},{"title":"Human in the loop","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#human-in-the-loop","text":"await human(question) asks a person and resolves to their answer as a string. Execution continues immediately after an answer, without a suspension and without repeating completed tool and model calls. Programs that embed the runtime pass a human(question, line) handler to run; without one, human fails with runtime_error. Node.js programs that use the library read stdin by default, or call the human option given to configure.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Resuming with a trace","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#resuming-with-a-trace","text":"The same --trace path automatically replays recorded decisions, tool results and answers, then continues interrupted work. --human answers only questions not yet recorded; other new questions are asked as usual. Completed runs replay the same result without prompts or repeating recorded effects. Choose another JSONL file to restart. The working directory, source, runtime and model/provider/MCP configuration must match. Arguments and accessed environment values are retained. External files are not restored: changes made between runs stay changed. An interrupted side effect with no recorded outcome requires manual recovery rather than an unsafe retry. See Traces and replay for corruption handling and privacy considerations.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"CLI & runtime","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html","text":"spl runs, checks, compiles and documents .spl workflows. A run prints its result on stdout and its logs on stderr, and reports the outcome through its exit code. Requires Node.js >=24.15.0.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Commands","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#commands","text":"Command Effect --- --- spl run \u003cfile.spl> Compile the workflow in memory and run it in place spl check \u003cfile.spl> Scan and type-check it, without running spl compile \u003cfile.spl> Write an importable TypeScript module beside the file spl doc \u003cfile.spl> Print its documentation, as Markdown or HTML spl login \u003cprovider> Save an API key and select the default provider spl logout [provider] Remove saved credentials; environment variables are unchanged spl --version , spl --help Version and usage, also -v and -h run, check, compile and doc take exactly one .spl file. A file name without a command is a usage error that suggests spl run.","group":"Reference","signatures":["spl run \u003cfile.spl>","spl check \u003cfile.spl>","spl compile \u003cfile.spl>","spl doc \u003cfile.spl>","spl login \u003cprovider>","spl logout [provider]","spl --version","spl --help"]},{"title":"spl run","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#spl-run","text":"Option Description --- --- --arg \u003cvalue> Script argument exposed as args[i] ; repeatable, also --arg=value --human \u003canswer> Answer a human question without prompting; repeatable in question order, also --human=answer --model \u003cid> Model for every call, overriding block settings, call options and file defaults --provider \u003cname> openai , anthropic or google ; model ids also accept provider/model --cwd \u003cdir> Base folder for relative tool paths, restrictions, agents and MCP servers (default: current) --log-level \u003clevel> error , info (default) or debug --silent No execution logs; human prompts still appear on stderr --debug Step through the workflow in the [terminal view](#stepping-with---debug) --timeout \u003cs> Per model call (default 300) --mcp \u003cfile.json> MCP servers, see [MCP servers](#mcp-servers) --trace \u003cfile.jsonl> Append execution history, automatically resume or replay the same file; see [Traces and replay](#traces-and-replay) Each --arg is one string; use --arg=--value for values starting with a dash. The file is compiled before anything runs: a syntax error in the code or in a block stops the run with exit code 2. Types are erased, not checked; use spl check for that. --cwd does not affect imports, which resolve from the file that contains them. Runs handle human calls and the agents' askHuman questions directly. In the terminal view you type the answer in its status box; otherwise the shell prints each question on stderr and reads one line from stdin, preserving whitespace except the line ending. Repeat --human to supply answers in question order without prompting: Supplied answers are exact strings, including empty or multiline values; use --human=--value for answers starting with a dash. Once supplied answers are exhausted, remaining questions prompt normally, even with --silent. Reaching the end of stdin without an answer is a failure of the workflow, not a suspension. With --trace, recorded answers replay without prompting or consuming supplied --human values; those values answer only new questions.","group":"Reference","signatures":["--arg \u003cvalue>","--human \u003canswer>","--model \u003cid>","--provider \u003cname>","--cwd \u003cdir>","--log-level \u003clevel>","--silent","--debug","--timeout \u003cs>","--mcp \u003cfile.json>","--trace \u003cfile.jsonl>"]},{"title":"spl check","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#spl-check","text":"spl check scans the workflow and every .spl file it imports, then type-checks them with the TypeScript compiler. Nothing runs and no model is called. Problems are printed on stderr as file:line:column: message, at positions of the .spl file, and the exit code is 2. A clean file prints nothing and exits with 0. It reports Example --- --- Scanner errors A misspelled header key, an unmatched --- , a key that does not apply to the mode Syntax errors in code A missing bracket, await in a function that is not async Type errors in code const total: number = 'ten' References that do not exist @notes when no notes is in scope: Cannot find name 'notes' Outputs used with the wrong type ->[Int] @=name when name is a string; a property that outputType does not have Imports that do not resolve A missing module or a missing export spl check cannot find errors that only appear at run time, such as an answer that does not fit its type.","group":"Reference","signatures":[]},{"title":"spl compile","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#spl-compile","text":"spl compile writes \u003cname>.ts beside the workflow, and beside every .spl file it imports with a relative path. It prints the paths it wrote. For imports/math.spl the module is:","group":"Reference"},{"title":"spl doc","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#spl-doc","text":"spl doc prints the workflow as a document: Markdown by default, one self-contained HTML page with --format html. In the file In the document --- --- A //* ... *// comment Prose, as written A run of top-level code between comments and blocks One ts code listing, imports and ordinary comments included A top-level block A callout headed by its mode, with its settings as a small table and its instruction as quoted text The file defaults A callout headed file defaults A block nested inside code Part of the code listing, as written For the greet.spl of Getting started it prints: The HTML format uses the styles and the syntax highlighter of this documentation site. A file with syntax problems is reported like in spl run, not documented. The Examples page is generated from the example files with spl doc.","group":"Reference","signatures":["//* ... *//"]},{"title":"Output and exit codes","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#output-and-exit-codes","text":"stdout gets the success value: strings as-is, everything else as JSON. A workflow that ends without calling success prints nothing. An uncaught failure prints its payload instead. stderr gets logs, including every model prompt and response, plus changes to variables with --log-level debug. Use --silent when logs could expose sensitive data. Each event has one header: Icon Category Color --- --- --- -> Model input: ask, agent or codegen Magenta \u003c- Model output or agent action Cyan x Error Red ! Warning Yellow i Information Blue . Debug Gray ~ Variable state Yellow * Tool call: * tool readFile path , followed by its content; failed on errors Green / red log(...) calls keep their [info|error|debug] log: prefix. Tool calls from code (readFile, writeFile, webFetch, webSearch, shell), from agent blocks, from agent() and from MCP servers are logged at info with what they acted on and their content: the file read or written, the fetched body, search results, command output, MCP result or error. Code Meaning --- --- 0 Success 1 Uncaught failure of the workflow 2 Syntax, type or usage error, including problems found by spl check and a trace that does not match 70 Internal or infrastructure error 130 Cancelled with Ctrl+C, or with e while stepping","group":"Reference","signatures":["->","\u003c-","x","!","i",".","~","*","0","1","2","70","130"]},{"title":"Terminal view","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#terminal-view","text":"When stdin and stderr are terminals, logs appear in a full-screen Ink view on the alternate screen of stderr, even when stdout is piped. A header names the workflow, the timeline follows new output, and a status box at the bottom shows a spinner while the workflow runs, a human question with its answer field, or the final outcome. The available keys are always listed under it. Item Shown as --- --- Model prompt ❯ ask prompt ; generated text such as guidance, restrictions and the instructions for agents is a collapsed ▸ preamble line, and the workflow's own prompt follows with a │ gutter Model response ● ask response with the text; generated code is a collapsed ▸ JavaScript · N lines Tool call ● readFile path (green) or ✗ (red) and a collapsed summary such as ▸ 42 lines Diagnostics ✗ error, ! warning, • info, · debug, ~ state, ▪ script log(...) Tool content, generated code, preambles and every object/array start collapsed. Up/Down selects a collapsed item, Space or Enter expands or collapses it, Page Up/Down scrolls and Home/End reach the ends. Until you navigate, the newest item is selected, so Space expands the latest tool call. Expanded files, fetched pages, search results and generated code appear as they would in a file: line numbers, preserved indentation and syntax colors for JavaScript, TypeScript, JSON and Markdown. Expanded JSON uses two-space indentation and preserves original numeric/string tokens and duplicate keys. Scrolling away from the bottom pauses following, not execution; End resumes following. Model responses and available reasoning stream live in this view by default; hide: true suppresses reasoning. The live preview is replaced by the complete response when it arrives. --silent also suppresses streaming previews. human() questions and the agents' askHuman questions are answered in the status box: type the answer and press Enter. Supplied --human answers are used first, without prompting. When the workflow ends, the view stays open with ✓ Completed or ✗ Failed until you press e. Exiting restores the terminal exactly as it was and prints nothing to stderr; stdout then gets only the result or failure payload. Ctrl+C cancels a running workflow (exit code 130) and also closes a finished one. If the view cannot start or stops unexpectedly, SPL reports it and continues with append-only logs without changing the workflow result. Non-terminal stdin/stderr, TERM=dumb, --silent, help and version, login and logout, check, compile and doc never start the view. Message, label and value controls are sanitized rather than executed as terminal instructions. Redirected logs are plain, append-only and fully expanded: JSON is indented, tool content and generated code follow their header without line-number gutters, and nothing waits for a key.","group":"Reference","signatures":[]},{"title":"Stepping with --debug","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#stepping-with---debug","text":"--debug stops the workflow before its first step and lets you run it one step at a time. A step is a --- block or a top-level statement. Blocks inside functions and loops are steps too; function declarations, imports and exported declarations are not. Key Action --- --- n Next: run the next step, then stop again v Variables: show the top-level variables as JSON c Continue: run to the end without stopping e Exit: stop the workflow immediately (exit code 130) Each stop adds a ▶ statement · line N or ▶ agent block · line N item with the step's source collapsed, and the status box shows the line it will run. v lists every top-level variable of the workflow; one that has not been assigned yet shows \u003cuninitialized>. --debug needs the terminal view, so it cannot be combined with --silent or used with redirected stdin or stderr.","group":"Reference","signatures":["n","v","c","e"]},{"title":"State logs","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#state-logs","text":"--log-level debug adds state events: every change to a top-level variable, with its previous and its new value.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Colors and levels","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#colors-and-levels","text":"Colors are enabled automatically when stderr is a terminal, and disabled for redirected output or TERM=dumb. Set NO_COLOR=1 to disable color, or FORCE_COLOR=1 to enable it when redirecting; FORCE_COLOR=0 disables color, and NO_COLOR takes precedence. stdout results and trace journals remain plain, machine-readable data. Forced color never enables cursor controls or keyboard input. --silent suppresses execution logs; usage, syntax and trace-usage diagnostics still appear on stderr so command-line errors are not hidden. --log-level filters by severity: model prompts and responses appear at every level that is not silent, tool calls from info, and state events only at debug. For example, log('review:', review) produces:","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Traces and replay","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#traces-and-replay","text":"--trace is the only persistence and replay option. A missing file starts a run. With an existing file, the workflow's code runs again from the top while every recorded effect returns its recorded result, which rebuilds variables, functions and loops; execution then continues live at the first unrecorded effect. Records are appended and flushed durably: existing bytes are never rewritten. A completed success or failure replays, prints the same result and leaves the trace unchanged. To restart, choose a different JSONL file. The JSONL journal records the effects of the library: model requests, raw responses and failures (including repairs, reasoning, sampling and token budgets), decision() answers, every decision of an agent block, readFile/writeFile/webFetch/webSearch/shell outcomes, restrictions, human answers, generated code, accessed environment values and the final result. Blocks go through the same journal as ask() and agent(). Completed effects are not executed again: replay never contacts model providers or MCP, re-reads files through the tools, repeats writes, commands or network calls, or prompts for recorded answers. It does not recreate side effects if their files have been removed or changed. Only library effects are replayed. Plain code is executed again on every run. Anything your code does through Node.js APIs directly, such as node:fs, fetch, child_process, Date.now() or Math.random(), is neither recorded nor suppressed. If such a value changes and flows into a prompt or tool argument, the request no longer matches the journal and the run stops with trace effect history mismatch. Imported modules are part of that plain code. They are loaded again on replay and their source is not recorded, whether they are .js helpers or other .spl files: an edited module can change the replayed result, and a deleted one makes the replay fail. The source of the workflow, the runtime, the working directory and the model/provider/MCP configuration must match the recording. Arguments are retained; omitting --arg reuses them, while explicitly different arguments fail. Requests must match exactly and in order; malformed, mismatched and unused history fail without falling back to live work. After an interruption, a recorded human question, a model call without tools, a decision(), a read or the next decision of an agent block can continue. If a write, a shell command or an agent() call was interrupted between its durable intent and outcome, its side effects may already have occurred: SPL reports an unresolved effect instead of repeating it. Truncated final records likewise require manual recovery; SPL never truncates the log to guess. Only one process can write a trace at a time; a lock from a terminated process is reclaimed. Calls that run concurrently, such as Promise.all(items.map(item => ask(...))), are recorded one at a time in call order when a trace is active, so they replay deterministically. Keep traces private. They include prompts, responses, human answers and tool results. Reading env, in code or as @env.NAME in a block, captures the environment for deterministic reconstruction; an agent's getEnv records only the requested value. These may contain secrets. New files use owner-only permissions where supported. A trace is not a transaction system or a sandbox.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"MCP servers","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#mcp-servers","text":"--mcp takes a file in VS Code's mcp.json format. MCP tools are available only to agents: agent blocks and agent() calls. The model sees each MCP tool under a camelCase name: mcp, the position of its server among the servers available to the call, and the tool's name in PascalCase. The search tool of the first server is mcp0Search, and search_docs of the second is mcp1SearchDocs. Logs and restrictions use the configured names instead: docs/search. Use the server names in restrict, as mcp.spl does. An invalid file is a usage error before the run starts. Only load configurations you trust.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Models and authentication","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#models-and-authentication","text":"Every model call uses the official OpenAI, Anthropic or Google client in a fresh conversation. decision() calls the OpenAI and Google decision endpoints with their fixed models (gpt-6-luna, gemini-decision-1) and simulates one on Anthropic. See Getting started for API-key login and environment variables. A call without credentials, or a provider/model access error, fails with llm_error. Workflows without model calls, check, compile, doc and replay do not initialize providers or MCP. Model precedence: --model, then the model of the block or call, then the file default, then the provider default; see Models and reasoning. Ids may be bare (claude-sonnet-4-5, gemini-2.5-flash, gpt-4.1) or qualified (anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5). Recognizable bare ids infer their provider; custom ids can be qualified or used with --provider. Unknown models are passed unchanged to the selected provider and fail if unavailable; there is no fallback to another model. Provider selection uses explicit --provider, then the model's prefix or inferred provider, then SPL_PROVIDER, then the saved login default, then a single configured provider. An explicit provider conflicting with the model's provider is an error. Multiple configured providers without a selection are an error, not an arbitrary choice. Defaults are gpt-4.1 (OpenAI), claude-sonnet-4-5 (Anthropic), and gemini-2.5-flash (Google). Tools run one at a time, MCP results remain traceable, and timeout or cancellation never accepts a partial answer. Failed actions are not automatically retried. Live output remains non-reproducible; contain variance with types, assertions and traces.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Runtime limits","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#runtime-limits","text":"","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Embedding","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#embedding","text":"The package that provides the CLI is also the library. Besides the workflow functions, it exports what is needed to run .spl source from a program. run compiles the source in memory and runs it once. Syntax problems throw SplSyntaxError (with problems, line and col) before anything runs. Otherwise it resolves to { status: 'success', value, hasValue } or { status: 'failure', failure }; hasValue is false when the workflow ended without success. run option Meaning --- --- backend , logger Required: the model backend and the log destination file Path of the workflow: its imports resolve from here, generated code is cached beside it and failures name it (default: workflow.spl in cwd ) cwd Working directory for relative tool paths, restrictions and tools (default: process directory) args Script arguments (default [] , never process.argv ) model Overrides file defaults and the models of blocks and calls, like --model human (question, line) => string or a promise of one; answers human and askHuman trace An open Trace to record and replay effects beforeStep Awaited before each --- block and each top-level statement; --debug pauses here Embedded runs never read stdin themselves; provide a human handler when a workflow asks for input. beforeStep receives { kind, line, language, source, variables() }: kind is code or block, language is ts, ask, agent or codegen, and variables() returns a JSON-shaped snapshot of the top-level variables. A compiled module is the other way to run a workflow from a program: import it and call its default export. Export Purpose --- --- scan(source) The front scanner: the generated TypeScript, the blocks, comments, file defaults and problems compile(source) Scan, erase types and analyse; throws SplSyntaxError check(file) What spl check does; returns the problems documentMarkdown(source, options?) , documentHtml(source, title) What spl doc prints HEADER_KEYS , MODES The header keys and block modes, for editors Trace , loadMcpConfig , login , logout , authStatus Traces, MCP configuration and credentials Use Trace.open(file, { source, runtime: 'your-runtime-id', cwd, args, configuration }) and pass the result as trace. After execution and backend cleanup, call trace.finish(result); always call trace.close() in finally. The same file automatically resumes or replays. A custom backend implements call(request) and close(), which makes offline tests simple: request.prompt is the fully assembled prompt and request.kind is ask or agent; only agent requests may run tools. For an agent block the request also has the runtimeTools definitions and invokeAction({ name, arguments }) to perform a tool call. AiBackend accepts an optional provider alongside logger, cwd, timeoutMs and mcpServers. Embedded callers can pass structured data to a logger directly with logger.info('review:', review). Custom logger sinks default to plain text; new Logger('info', sink, { color: true }) explicitly enables styling. An eventSink option delivers detached read-only events instead of strings, and a streamSink receives live model previews. Neither library imports nor custom sinks load the terminal view or read input.","group":"Reference","signatures":["backend","logger","file","cwd","args","model","human","trace","beforeStep","scan(source)","compile(source)","check(file)","documentMarkdown(source, options?)","documentHtml(source, title)","HEADER_KEYS","MODES","Trace","loadMcpConfig","login","logout","authStatus"]},{"title":"API reference","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html","text":"Everything a workflow can use on one page: the file format, every header key, the errors the scanner reports, every library global with its signature and options, the type builder, the agent tools and the failure codes.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"File format","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#file-format","text":"A workflow is a .spl file: TypeScript with Markdown comments and blocks. Construct Treatment --- --- //* ... *// , each on a line of its own [Markdown comment](Language.md#markdown-comments): never runs; prose in spl doc . Top level only --- ... --- with an instruction [Block](Language.md#blocks): an instruction for a model, with an optional header --- ... --- with header lines only [File defaults](Language.md#file-defaults); must be the first item that is not a comment Everything else TypeScript with [erasable syntax](Language.md#the-typescript-dialect), run as an ES module A block may stand wherever a statement may. Blocks, references and outputs compile to calls on the lines they occupy; see How a file is compiled.","group":"Reference","signatures":["//*","*//","---","---","---","---"]},{"title":"Header keys","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#header-keys","text":"Key Modes File default Value --- --- --- --- mode all No ask (default), agent or codegen model all Yes Model id; provider/model selects a provider reasoning all Yes none , low , medium (default), high or max enforce all Yes Guidance the model must follow: a string or a list of strings forbid all Yes Guidance on what the model must avoid: a string or a list of strings hide all Yes true or false : hide reasoning in logs tokens all Yes Maximum generated tokens per model turn: a positive integer restrict agent No A folder, a list of folders, or { fs, exec, net, mcp } with strings or lists of strings outputVar ask No Name of the output variable; =name assigns an existing let . Required outputType ask No Type expression the answer is converted to; Str by default. TypeScript, not YAML repairs ask No Retries when the answer cannot be converted: an integer from 0 to 5 seed ask No Sampling seed: an integer from 0 to 2147483647 temperature ask No Sampling temperature: a number from 0 to 2 topP ask No Nucleus sampling: a number from 0 to 1 Values are YAML, except outputType. The header ends at a blank line or at the first line whose key is not in this table. See The header.","group":"Reference","signatures":["mode","model","reasoning","enforce","forbid","hide","tokens","restrict","outputVar","outputType","repairs","seed","temperature","topP"]},{"title":"References and outputs","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#references-and-outputs","text":"Written in a block Mode Meaning --- --- --- @name , @name.property all An input: a variable, function or type of the surrounding code @env.NAME all An environment variable @args all The script arguments @@ all A literal @ outputVar: name ask Declares the constant name outputVar: =name ask Assigns the existing variable name ->[Type] @name , -> @name agent Declares an output, typed or untyped ->[Type] @=name , -> @=name agent Assigns the existing variable name -> @name(params): Type , -> @name codegen Declares a generated function, with or without a signature A reference starts a word: user@example.com is literal text. There is no ${...} interpolation.","group":"Reference","signatures":["@name","@name.property","@env.NAME","@args","@@","outputVar: name","outputVar: =name","->[Type] @name","-> @name","->[Type] @=name","-> @=name","-> @name(params): Type","-> @name"]},{"title":"Errors the scanner reports","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#errors-the-scanner-reports","text":"Syntax problems are found before anything runs. spl run, spl compile and spl doc print them as file:line:column: syntax error: message and exit with code 2; spl check prints them without the syntax error: label. Situation Error --- --- Odd number of --- fences Unmatched block opened at line N: every --- needs a closing --- Block in expression position Blocks are statements: a --- block cannot be used as an expression //* below top level or inside a block Markdown comments ( //* ) are only allowed at the top level Unclosed //* Unclosed Markdown comment: //* needs a closing *// at the start of a line Text on the line of *// *// must be alone on its line Misspelled header key unknown header key modle ; did you mean model ? Header key not valid for the mode outputVar applies only to ask blocks , restrict applies only to agent blocks Ask block without outputVar ask block needs outputVar: name the variable that receives the answer, such as outputVar: summary Ask block with -> ask block needs outputVar: -> declares outputs only in agent and codegen blocks Header-only block that is not first File defaults must come first: a block without an instruction sets defaults for the whole file Key that cannot be a file default mode cannot be a file default; it belongs to a block with an instruction Block with no header and no instruction empty block: write an instruction between the --- lines Header key set twice header key model is set twice Header value that is not valid YAML enforce is not valid YAML: followed by the parser's message outputType without a value outputType needs a type expression such as Int or List(Str) Agent output declared twice output @notes is declared twice in this block Codegen block without a function codegen block needs a function to generate, such as -> @slugify(text: string): string @=name in a codegen block generated functions are always new constants; write @name , not @=name 2.x syntax SPL 2.x syntax; see the migration guide ; see [Migrating from 2.x](Migration.md) enum Unsupported TypeScript syntax: enum cannot be erased; use a union of string literals instead of enum namespace or module with a body Unsupported TypeScript syntax: namespace cannot be erased; use an ES module or a plain object instead of namespace Decorator Unsupported TypeScript syntax: decorators cannot be erased; call the decorator as a function instead Constructor parameter property Unsupported TypeScript syntax: parameter properties cannot be erased; declare the field and assign it in the constructor A header value of the wrong kind names the key and what it accepts: Key Error --- --- mode mode must be ask, agent or codegen model model must be a model id reasoning reasoning must be none, low, medium, high or max enforce , forbid enforce must be a string or a list of strings hide hide must be true or false tokens tokens must be a positive integer restrict restrict must be a folder or { fs, exec, net, mcp } with strings or lists of strings outputVar outputVar must be a variable name, or =name to assign an existing variable repairs repairs must be an integer from 0 to 5 seed seed must be an integer from 0 to 2147483647 temperature temperature must be a number from 0 to 2 topP topP must be a number from 0 to 1 After the scanner, the generated TypeScript is erased and parsed. Problems found there are reported the same way: Situation Error --- --- Code that is not valid TypeScript invalid TypeScript: followed by the parser's message Other syntax that cannot be erased Unsupported TypeScript syntax: followed by the detail and ; only type syntax that can be erased is allowed export default the default export of a .spl file is its workflow; remove export default Exported declaration that uses a workflow variable exported double uses the workflow variable base ; exports live outside the workflow, so export base too or pass it as a parameter export { name } of a workflow variable cannot export the workflow variable base ; write export on its declaration instead Type errors, including references to names that do not exist, are reported by spl check with TypeScript's own messages.","group":"Reference","signatures":["---","//*","//*","*//","outputVar","->","outputType","@=name","enum","namespace","module","mode","model","reasoning","enforce","forbid","hide","tokens","restrict","outputVar","repairs","seed","temperature","topP","export default","export { name }"]},{"title":"Model calls","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#model-calls","text":"Call Resolves to --- --- ask(prompt, options?) The answer; no tools agent(prompt, options?) The answer after the model acted with the exact tools and MCP tools ask Text ${value} Tagged-template form of both calls, without options A prompt that is not a string is converted to text: objects and arrays as JSON, types by name. Option Type Default Calls --- --- --- --- len 'word' , 'sentence' , 'paragraph' , 'long' none all fmt 'text' , 'integer' , 'float' , 'boolean' , 'array' , 'object' , 'json' 'text' , or 'json' with schema all schema A type or JSON Schema (object or boolean) none all repairs Integer 0 to 5 0 ask reasoning 'none' , 'low' , 'medium' , 'high' , 'max' File default, else 'medium' ; 'none' when sampling is set all hide Boolean File default, else false all model Nonempty string File default, else the provider's all tokens Positive safe integer File default, else the provider's all seed Integer 0 to 2147483647 provider default ask temperature Number 0 to 2 provider default ask topP Number 0 to 1 provider default ask The result is a string for text output, the parsed JSON value for fmt, and the converted value of the type for schema. In TypeScript the return type follows the options. Invalid options are a runtime_error raised before any model call. See Options.","group":"Reference","signatures":["ask(prompt, options?)","agent(prompt, options?)","ask`Text ${value}`","len","fmt","schema","repairs","reasoning","hide","model","tokens","seed","temperature","topP"]},{"title":"Decisions","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#decisions","text":"Call Resolves to --- --- decision({ question, evidence?, cutoff?, model? }) true when the probability of yes is above cutoff decision({ question, evidence?, labels, model? }) The boolean of the chosen label Option Type Default --- --- --- question Nonempty string required evidence String, or a list of values shown as text or JSON none cutoff Number 0 to 1 ; not with labels 0.5 labels Object of 2 to 255 labels mapped to true or false , with at least one of each none model Nonempty string; selects the provider File default, else the provider's OpenAI and Google use their decision endpoints with fixed models, gpt-6-luna and gemini-decision-1; Anthropic models simulate a decision with ask. See Decisions.","group":"Reference","signatures":["decision({ question, evidence?, cutoff?, model? })","decision({ question, evidence?, labels, model? })","question","evidence","cutoff","labels","model"]},{"title":"Guidance and restrictions","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#guidance-and-restrictions","text":"Call Effect --- --- enforce(text, body) Runs body with positive guidance added to every model call inside; returns its result forbid(text, body) Runs body with guidance about what to avoid; returns its result restrict(spec, body) Runs body with narrowed permissions; resolves to its result text is a nonempty string or a list of them. body is a function, usually an arrow function. spec is a folder, a list of folders, or an object: spec key Limits Values --- --- --- fs Files read and written by tools and agents Folders or files, relative to the working directory exec Executables run by shell Names without a path, such as git net Hosts reached by webFetch , webSearch and agents Host names; *.example.com matches subdomains mcp MCP tools offered to agents server , server/* or server/tool Each value is a comma-separated string or a list of strings. See Restrictions.","group":"Reference","signatures":["enforce(text, body)","forbid(text, body)","restrict(spec, body)","fs","exec","net","mcp"]},{"title":"Control, people and logging","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#control-people-and-logging","text":"Call Effect --- --- success(value?) Ends the workflow with a result ( null when omitted) fail(reason?) Raises explicit_failure ; the default reason is failed assert(condition, message?) Raises assertion_failed when condition is falsy human(question) Resolves to a person's answer as a string log(...values) , log.info(...) Writes to the run log at info level log.error(...values) , log.debug(...values) Writes at error or debug level Failure The class of failures: error instanceof Failure , statusCode , status_code , reason , line , command , payload()","group":"Reference","signatures":["success(value?)","fail(reason?)","assert(condition, message?)","human(question)","log(...values)","log.info(...)","log.error(...values)","log.debug(...values)","Failure"]},{"title":"Tools","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#tools","text":"Call Resolves to --- --- readFile(path) The file's UTF-8 text writeFile(path, content) A confirmation; parent folders are created webFetch(url) The body of an http(s) GET webSearch(query) The top results as text shell(command) Combined stdout and stderr Arguments are strings. Failures are tool_error; blocked calls are restriction_violation. Tool names are camelCase everywhere: in code, as the tools an agent sees and in logs.","group":"Reference","signatures":["readFile(path)","writeFile(path, content)","webFetch(url)","webSearch(query)","shell(command)"]},{"title":"Inputs and code generation","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#inputs-and-code-generation","text":"Name Value --- --- args Read-only array of --arg values env Read-only environment variables; access is recorded for replay codegen(description, options?) Resolves to a generated function; options are model , reasoning , hide and tokens","group":"Reference","signatures":["args","env","codegen(description, options?)"]},{"title":"Types","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#types","text":"Builder Kind JSON Schema --- --- --- Str str { \"type\": \"string\" } Int int { \"type\": \"integer\" } Float float { \"type\": \"number\" } Bool bool { \"type\": \"boolean\" } Char char String with minLength and maxLength of 1 Nil null { \"type\": \"null\" } Any any {} Dict dict { \"type\": \"object\" } Fn fn None: describing it is a runtime_error List(T) list { \"type\": \"array\", \"items\": T } Union(A, B, ...) union { \"anyOf\": [A, B, ...] } Nullable(T) union { \"anyOf\": [T, { \"type\": \"null\" }] } Enum('a', 'b') , Enum(['a', 'b']) enum { \"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"a\", \"b\"] } Struct(name, fields?) struct Object with every field in required and additionalProperties: false Typedef(name, type, annotations?) Kind of type Schema of type plus annotations JsonSchema(schema, name?) schema The given schema Member of every type Result --- --- kind , name , annotations The kind above, the display name and the attached annotations annotate(annotations) A copy with the annotations merged in describe(text) Shorthand for annotate({ description: text }) as(name, annotations?) A named copy; Typedef is the function form nullable() , list() , or(other) Union(T, Nil) , List(T) , Union(T, other) parse(value) The validated value; throws validation_failed with a path such as $.lines[0].total coerce(value) The value converted to the type, then validated; see [Parse and coerce](Language.md#parse-and-coerce) parseJson(text) parse of strict JSON text; throws invalid_format for malformed JSON is(value) Boolean schema() JSON Schema with annotations in description Member of a struct Result --- --- fields The field types by name field(name, type, annotations?) A copy with one more field methods({ ... }) A class type whose parsed values have these methods class(Constructor) A class type whose parsed values are instances; a static fromJSON(data) builds them when present data The plain-struct view of a class type validate(value, type) equals type.parse(value); validate(value, schema) checks raw JSON Schema and returns the value. t holds every builder under one name: t.Str, t.List, t.Struct. Type names in messages follow the builders: expected Int, got \"x\", List(Str), Str | Nil. TypeScript code gets the parsed type with Infer\u003ctypeof Type>, a global in .spl files and an export of spl-lang. See Types.","group":"Reference","signatures":["Str","Int","Float","Bool","Char","Nil","Any","Dict","Fn","List(T)","Union(A, B, ...)","Nullable(T)","Enum('a', 'b')","Enum(['a', 'b'])","Struct(name, fields?)","Typedef(name, type, annotations?)","JsonSchema(schema, name?)","kind","name","annotations","annotate(annotations)","describe(text)","as(name, annotations?)","nullable()","list()","or(other)","parse(value)","coerce(value)","parseJson(text)","is(value)","schema()","fields","field(name, type, annotations?)","methods({ ... })","class(Constructor)","data"]},{"title":"Markdown builder","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#markdown-builder","text":"markdown.document() (or new markdown.Document()) has the chainable methods heading, paragraph, bulletList, orderedList, quote, codeBlock, table, thematicBreak, raw and toString. markdown.bold, italic, code, link, image and escape return inline strings. See Markdown builder.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Agent tools","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#agent-tools","text":"The tools of an agent block: Tool Arguments Result --- --- --- loadVariable name The value of an input; a type's JSON Schema saveVariable name , value The saved value, or a rejection with a hint when it does not fit the output's type callFunction name , args (list) The function's result getEnv name The value, or null askHuman question The answer setGuidance kind ( enforce or forbid ), text Adds guidance to model calls made by functions called later in the block readFile , writeFile , webFetch , webSearch , shell As the tools above As the tools above mcp\u003cIndex>\u003cTool> The MCP tool's own The MCP result; see [MCP servers](CLI.md#mcp-servers) name is an identifier, optionally followed by .property parts, as written after @ in the instruction. loadVariable and callFunction accept only names the instruction references; saveVariable accepts only declared outputs. An agent() call in code has the exact tools and MCP tools only.","group":"Reference","signatures":["loadVariable","saveVariable","callFunction","getEnv","askHuman","setGuidance","readFile","writeFile","webFetch","webSearch","shell","mcp\u003cIndex>\u003cTool>"]},{"title":"Failure codes","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#failure-codes","text":"status_code Raised by --- --- invalid_format fmt and JSON parsing of answers, parseJson , invalid generated code validation_failed Types and raw JSON Schema: outputType , schema , parse , coerce , agent outputs assertion_failed assert , the Markdown builder explicit_failure fail restriction_violation Tools, agents and MCP inside restrict tool_error readFile , writeFile , webFetch , webSearch , shell , MCP tools llm_error Providers: credentials, access, timeouts, token limits, rejected settings runtime_error Uncaught JavaScript exceptions, invalid options and arguments, misuse of the library See Failure payload for the JSON shape and Output and exit codes for the exit codes.","group":"Reference","signatures":["invalid_format","validation_failed","assertion_failed","explicit_failure","restriction_violation","tool_error","llm_error","runtime_error"]},{"title":"Package entry points","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#package-entry-points","text":"Import Contents --- --- spl-lang The library globals, configure , the type classes and Infer , plus the embedding API: run , scan , compile , check , documentMarkdown , documentHtml , Runtime , Logger , AiBackend , Trace , loadMcpConfig , HEADER_KEYS , MODES and their types. __spl is what compiled workflows call spl-lang/markdown The Markdown builder: Document , document , bold , italic , code , link , image , escape spl-lang/globals Types only: the globals of a .spl file as ambient declarations Inside a workflow, in .spl files and in the modules they import, spl-lang and spl-lang/markdown always resolve to the running copy of the package: no local install is needed, and helpers share the runtime and the Failure class. To type-check your own helper modules with the globals of a .spl file, add them to a jsconfig.json or tsconfig.json:","group":"Reference","signatures":["spl-lang","spl-lang/markdown","spl-lang/globals"]},{"title":"Maintaining this page","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#maintaining-this-page","text":"The globals listed here are the keys of library() in lang/src/library.ts, which lang/globals.d.ts and the VS Code catalog mirror. The header keys are HEADER_KEYS in lang/src/scanner.ts, which also holds every scanner message. When they change, update this page with them and run npm run docs:build from the repository root. The documentation tests compile every spl listing and keep embedded example files in sync.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Examples","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html","text":"Every example is a runnable .spl file in lang/examples. Run them from the lang folder, for example spl run examples/hi.spl: the relative paths they read and write resolve against the working directory. The first lines of each example show its command and arguments. This page is generated from the files with spl doc. Comments are the prose, top-level code is listed as written, and each top-level block is a callout with its mode, its settings and its instruction. Check any of them without a model: spl check examples/hi.spl.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Getting started","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#getting-started","text":"Blocks, references, typed outputs and human input, a few lines at a time.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Hello, SPL","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#hello-spl","text":"A .spl file is TypeScript. Markdown comments like this one are commentary, and each --- block is an instruction for a model. Everything runs top to bottom, and the library (ask, Struct, writeFile, markdown, ...) needs no import. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna enforce Be concise. The agent reads cards through a tool and must save a value that fits Card. agent block Setting Value --- --- reasoning high Pick the item of @cards whose card a beginner would find hardest ->[Card] @hardest Source: hi.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model","enforce","reasoning"]},{"title":"Random article word frequencies","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#random-article-word-frequencies","text":"An agent fetches a random longform article as Markdown. Plain code then counts how often each word appears, so the counts are exact. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna agent block Get a random article from https://longform.org/random.It will give you page with a random article link,navigate to it and return its contents as markdown.Save to ->[Str] @article Source: random-article.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model"]},{"title":"Fibonacci check","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#fibonacci-check","text":"This a markdown comment. It is rendered as prose by spl doc. Points: file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna agent block Setting Value --- --- reasoning medium Invoke @factorial with 15 as argument ->[Int] @fact15Invoke @fib with 10 as argument ->[Int] @fib10Add 2 to @fib10 ->[Int] @fibPlus2Work out the 10th Fibonacci number yourself, without using @fib ->[Int] @modelFib10 ask block Setting Value --- --- outputVar joke outputType Joke enforce Answer in Chinese forbid Mentioning the word cat A joke about programmers with a setup and a punchline. ask block Setting Value --- --- outputVar animal A paragraph of a funny story about a pet animal. Source: basics.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model","reasoning","outputVar","outputType","enforce","forbid","outputVar"]},{"title":"Is it Friday?","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#is-it-friday","text":"outputType: Bool makes the answer a real boolean, so code can branch on it. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna ask block Setting Value --- --- outputVar isFriday outputType Bool Today is @today. Is it Friday? ask block Setting Value --- --- outputVar complaint Complain in an original way that it is not Friday yet. Source: is-friday.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model","outputVar","outputType","outputVar"]},{"title":"Environment variables and arguments","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#environment-variables-and-arguments","text":"In a block, @env.NAME reads an environment variable and @args reads the arguments. Code reads env and args directly. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna ask block Setting Value --- --- outputVar sum outputType Int Calculate the sum of @env.lhs and @env.rhs Source: env.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model","outputVar","outputType"]},{"title":"Ask a person, then a model","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#ask-a-person-then-a-model","text":"human() asks in the terminal; --human \"navy blue\" answers without prompting. Source: rgb.spl","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Name a color","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#name-a-color","text":"The settings of a block are the lines of its header. agent block Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna reasoning low Find the common name for @rgbString.Don't search the web, give an approximation ->[Str] @colorName Source: rgb2.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model","reasoning"]},{"title":"A poem from two questions","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#a-poem-from-two-questions","text":"An agent may ask a person with its askHuman tool. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna agent block Ask the human \"What is your favorite color?\" ->[Str] @colorAsk the human \"What is your favorite fruit?\" ->[Str] @fruit ask block Setting Value --- --- outputVar poem A paragraph long poem about @color and @fruit Source: color-poem.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model","outputVar"]},{"title":"Check an untyped answer in code","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#check-an-untyped-answer-in-code","text":"An output without a type is saved as plain JSON, typed unknown: code decides what to make of it. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna reasoning low agent block Give an answer to everything as an integer -> @response Source: answer.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model","reasoning"]},{"title":"Control flow and guidance","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#control-flow-and-guidance","text":"Blocks inside if and loops, outputs that assign existing variables, decisions, guidance and sampling.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Conditional workflows","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#conditional-workflows","text":"Blocks are statements, so they can sit inside if and while. An output written as =name assigns an existing variable instead of declaring a new one. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna reasoning low Source: conditional-workflows.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model","reasoning"]},{"title":"Retry until the answer checks out","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#retry-until-the-answer-checks-out","text":"A plain loop asks, checks the answer in code and tells the model what was wrong. SPL 2.x needed go() for this. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna Source: goto.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model"]},{"title":"Decisions","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#decisions","text":"decision() answers a question about the evidence with a boolean, so code can branch on it. With cutoff, it is true when the probability of yes is above the cutoff; with labels, the model picks a label and the result is the boolean that label stands for. OpenAI and Google answer with their decision endpoints, whatever the model below; Anthropic models simulate one. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna Source: decision.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model"]},{"title":"Guidance in code and in blocks","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#guidance-in-code-and-in-blocks","text":"enforce() adds an instruction to every model call made inside its callback. The enforce header key does the same for one block. ask block Setting Value --- --- outputVar polishAnswer model gpt-6-luna enforce Answer in Polish Translate this story: @answer Source: enforce.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["outputVar","model","enforce"]},{"title":"Randomness controls","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#randomness-controls","text":"seed, temperature and topP limit randomness in an ask block. With sampling set, reasoning defaults to none. Sampling is best-effort and does not guarantee identical responses. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna ask block Setting Value --- --- outputVar similar outputType Bool Are the two recipes in @recipes semantically at least 95% similar? Source: randomness.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model","outputVar","outputType"]},{"title":"No model required","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#no-model-required","text":"These run offline, without credentials.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Typed data without a model","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#typed-data-without-a-model","text":"Types are built with library functions. This workflow uses no model calls or credentials: It covers primitives, named aliases, enums, exact structs, nullable fields, unions, annotations, classes with methods, validation, coercion and JSON Schema output. With a model, outputType: Person in an ask block coerces the answer into the same objects. Annotations are advice for the model; use assertions for business rules. Source: typed-data.spl","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Build Markdown without a model","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#build-markdown-without-a-model","text":"The markdown builder is part of the library, so this workflow needs no model or login. Source: markdown.spl","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Plain TypeScript and the Node.js standard library","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#plain-typescript-and-the-nodejs-standard-library","text":"Outside its blocks a workflow is TypeScript: top-level await, imports and every Node.js built-in work as usual. Source: deterministic/node.spl","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Enums and structs","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#enums-and-structs","text":"Source: deterministic/enumsStructs.spl","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Named types and annotations","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#named-types-and-annotations","text":"Source: deterministic/types.spl","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Import modules, Node.js built-ins and other workflows","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#import-modules-nodejs-built-ins-and-other-workflows","text":"Imports resolve from this file, as in any ES module. Another .spl file gives its exported declarations by name and its workflow as the default export. Source: imports/run.spl","group":"Guides"},{"title":"A workflow that is also a module","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#a-workflow-that-is-also-a-module","text":"Exported declarations live at module level, so other files can import them. The rest of the file is the workflow: await squares(['2', '3']) runs it with those arguments. Source: imports/math.spl","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Developer workflows","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#developer-workflows","text":"Agents and exact tools around a repository: reviews, release notes, issue triage and generated code.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Review the riskiest source files","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#review-the-riskiest-source-files","text":"An agent picks and reviews three files inside ./src, an ask block turns its notes into typed findings, and plain code decides whether the run fails. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna agent block Setting Value --- --- restrict {\"fs\":\"./src\",\"exec\":\"git\"} forbid suggesting purely stylistic changes List source files with git ls-files src.Then read the 3 files that are most likely to contain security problems or correctness bugs,and review only those. List each finding with its location, why it matters and a suggested fix -> @notes ask block Setting Value --- --- outputVar review outputType Review Convert these review notes into structured findings: @notes agent block Setting Value --- --- restrict {\"fs\":\"./reports\"} model gpt-6.1-sol Convert @groups to a Markdown report and write it to './reports/review.md' Source: code-review.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model","restrict","forbid","outputVar","outputType","restrict","model"]},{"title":"Release notes from git history","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#release-notes-from-git-history","text":"Collects commits since the latest tag, writes user-facing notes and prepends them to CHANGELOG.md. Pass the new version as the first argument. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna ask block Setting Value --- --- outputVar draft enforce Write for users of the product: describe the impact, not the implementation forbid inventing changes that are not in the commits, and listing internal refactors or CI tweaks Group @commits into the sections \"Features\", \"Fixes\" and \"Other\" (omit empty sections) andwrite Markdown release notes for @version. Start with the heading ## \u003cversion>, use ### for sectionsand one bullet per change. Source: release-notes.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model","outputVar","enforce","forbid"]},{"title":"Triage unlabeled GitHub issues","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#triage-unlabeled-github-issues","text":"Classifies open issues without labels and applies the labels only after a human approves the plan. Needs an authenticated gh CLI. Pass --arg owner/repo to target another repository. Enums constrain labels and priorities, and a named list type receives the whole batch. Field annotations are advice, so code checks nonempty reasons. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna ask block Setting Value --- --- outputVar plan outputType TriagePlan forbid judging an issue by its title alone when the body says otherwise repairs 1 Classify every issue in @issues with exactly one label from @LABELS, a priority and a one-sentence reason.Crashes, data loss and security problems are high priority. agent block Show the @table lines and ask the human \"Apply these labels? (yes/no)\" ->[Str] @approved Source: triage-issues.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model","outputVar","outputType","forbid","repairs"]},{"title":"Generate a function, then use it","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#generate-a-function-then-use-it","text":"A codegen block asks the model for JavaScript functions and declares them as typed constants. The code is cached next to this file, as codegen.sieve.js, and reused on later runs. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna codegen block Sieve of Eratosthenes that returns the primes up to a given number -> @sieve(limit: number): number[] Source: codegen.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model"]},{"title":"Repository agentic operations","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#repository-agentic-operations","text":"An agent reads the repository it runs in and reports what it finds as typed data; plain code decides what to do about it. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna agent block Read the repository you are in and list its security vulnerabilities,grouped by severity ->[Vulnerabilities] @vulnerabilities Source: repo.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model"]},{"title":"Documents and data","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#documents-and-data","text":"Typed extraction and generation, with code checking what the model returned.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Extract an invoice into accounting-ready JSON","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#extract-an-invoice-into-accounting-ready-json","text":"Turns a pasted or OCR'd invoice into structured data. Monetary amounts use integer cents. The output type checks the shape; exact arithmetic checks the numbers, because a model can produce valid JSON with wrong totals. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna ask block Setting Value --- --- outputVar invoice outputType Invoice forbid filling in values that are not printed on the invoice repairs 1 Extract the invoice in @text. Dates are ISO YYYY-MM-DD, currency is an ISO 4217 code,and there is one entry per line item. Convert printed monetary amounts to integer cents(for example, 12.50 becomes 1250). Source: extract-invoice.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model","outputVar","outputType","forbid","repairs"]},{"title":"Types","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#types","text":"This sample shows how to describe model output with types, in a block and in a function call. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna ask block Setting Value --- --- outputVar person outputType Person A sample person Source: types.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model","outputVar","outputType"]},{"title":"Translate product docs without breaking them","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#translate-product-docs-without-breaking-them","text":"Translates a Markdown announcement into several languages, then verifies deterministically that links, e-mail addresses, code spans and the heading structure survived. Target languages come from the arguments. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna ask block Setting Value --- --- outputVar translations outputType Dict enforce Keep the Markdown structure, URLs, e-mail addresses and code spans exactly as in the source , Use the product's informal, friendly tone and localize UI paths such as menu names Translate the Markdown in @source into every language code in @languages.Return an object that maps each language code to its translated Markdown. Source: translate-docs.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model","outputVar","outputType","enforce"]},{"title":"Incident report from application logs","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#incident-report-from-application-logs","text":"Parsing and counting are plain code, so the numbers in the report are exact. The model only writes the narrative, from the extracted facts rather than the raw log. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna ask block Setting Value --- --- outputVar report forbid speculating beyond what the errors and counts show; mark guesses as hypotheses Write a blameless incident report in Markdown for errors between @first and @last.Use the sections \"Summary\", \"Timeline\", \"Impact\", \"Likely root cause\" and \"Follow-ups\",based on @errors and @counts. Source: incident-report.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model","outputVar","forbid"]},{"title":"Research and operations","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#research-and-operations","text":"The web, MCP servers, state between runs and a person in the loop.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Research digest with checked citations","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#research-digest-with-checked-citations","text":"Searches the web for a topic, reads the top results, and writes a short digest. Page fetches are restricted to the hosts of the chosen results, and every citation must point to a page that was actually fetched. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna ask block Setting Value --- --- outputVar urls outputType List(Str) From these search results pick up to 3 URLs of primary sources (official docs, specs, project blogs)over aggregators: @results ask block Setting Value --- --- outputVar digest forbid facts that are not supported by the pages Write a Markdown digest about @topic from @pages: a 3-sentence overview, then up to 5 bullet pointswith key facts. End each bullet with its source URL in square brackets. Source: web-digest.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model","outputVar","outputType","outputVar","forbid"]},{"title":"Answer questions from an MCP docs server","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#answer-questions-from-an-mcp-docs-server","text":"Needs an MCP configuration with a server named docs that exposes a search tool. MCP tools are available only to agents; restrict: { mcp } limits which ones a block may call. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna agent block Setting Value --- --- restrict {\"mcp\":\"docs/search\"} Use docs/search to answer @questionEnd with a line \"Sources:\" followed by the URLs of the pages you used ->[Str] @answer ask block Setting Value --- --- outputVar summary forbid adding facts that are not in the answer Rewrite this answer as at most five short bullet points followed by its sources: @answer Source: mcp.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model","restrict","outputVar","forbid"]},{"title":"Rolling journal","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#rolling-journal","text":"Reads and writes its state explicitly in journal.json. Initialize the file once: Reuse that trace to resume or replay the same run; use a different trace for the next journal entry. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna ask block Setting Value --- --- outputVar today Summarize these notes in one sentence: @notes Source: journal.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model","outputVar"]},{"title":"Human review","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#human-review","text":"Drafts an announcement, asks a reviewer directly in the shell, then applies the feedback. Supply --human to answer without prompting: To record and automatically resume the same run: Completed runs replay without prompts or side effects. Choose another JSONL file to restart. file defaults Setting Value --- --- model gpt-6-luna ask block Setting Value --- --- outputVar draft Draft a two-sentence English release announcement for these changes: @changes Source: human-review.spl","group":"Guides","signatures":["model","outputVar"]},{"title":"Migrating from 2.x","page":"Migrating from 2.x","href":"migration.html","text":"SPL 3.0 has one file format: a .spl file of TypeScript with Markdown comments and --- blocks. Runnable Markdown documents, the block tags of 2.x .spl files, control lines and go() are gone. This guide maps each 2.x construct to its 3.0 form and lists the behavior that changed. Migration is manual: there is no automatic converter and no compatibility mode.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Before and after","page":"Migrating from 2.x","href":"migration.html#before-and-after","text":"A 2.x Markdown workflow: The same workflow in 3.0: A 2.x .spl file with tags, control lines and go(): conditional-workflows.spl (2.x) In 3.0:","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Files and commands","page":"Migrating from 2.x","href":"migration.html#files-and-commands","text":"SPL 2.x SPL 3.0 --- --- workflow.md with frontmatter workflow.spl workflow.spl with {js} and {agent} tags workflow.spl with code and --- blocks spl workflow.md , spl workflow.spl spl run workflow.spl spl workflow.md --check spl check workflow.spl , which now type-checks too spl workflow.md --pin , then spl workflow.locked.md spl run workflow.spl --trace run.jsonl , twice: a completed trace replays without a model No equivalent spl compile workflow.spl : an importable TypeScript module No equivalent spl doc workflow.spl : the workflow as a document Notebooks in VS Code Removed; use the Run and Debug commands, or spl run --debug Every other option of spl run is unchanged: --arg, --human, --model, --provider, --cwd, --mcp, --timeout, --trace, --log-level, --silent and --debug.","group":"Guides","signatures":["workflow.md","workflow.spl","{js}","{agent}","spl workflow.md","spl workflow.spl","spl workflow.md --check","spl workflow.md --pin","spl workflow.locked.md"]},{"title":"File structure","page":"Migrating from 2.x","href":"migration.html#file-structure","text":"SPL 2.x SPL 3.0 --- --- Frontmatter extension and version Removed: the file extension identifies a workflow Frontmatter model: 'gpt-6-luna' [File defaults](Language.md#file-defaults) block with model: gpt-6-luna Frontmatter preamble: 'Be concise.' File defaults with enforce: Be concise. Headings and prose; {comment} ... {/comment} A [Markdown comment](Language.md#markdown-comments): //* ... *// A js fence; {js} ... {/js} Code, written directly in the file A ts fence; {ts} ... {/ts} The same: every file is TypeScript A fence marked ignore or no-run ; {js ignore} An indented or fenced listing inside a Markdown comment A blockquote; {agent} ... {/agent} An [agent block](LLM-commands.md#agent-blocks), or an [ask block](LLM-commands.md#ask-blocks) when one answer is all it produces @CTL(...) at the start of a blockquote; settings in an {agent ...} tag [Header keys](LLM-commands.md#the-header) @CODEGEN 'description' ; {codegen} ... {/codegen} A [codegen block](LLM-commands.md#codegen-blocks) The journal of a locked workflow; {lock} Removed with locked workflows","group":"Guides","signatures":["extension","version","model: 'gpt-6-luna'","preamble: 'Be concise.'","{comment}","{/comment}","js","{js}","{/js}","ts","{ts}","{/ts}","ignore","no-run","{js ignore}","{agent}","{/agent}","@CTL(...)","{agent ...}","@CODEGEN 'description'","{codegen}","{/codegen}","{lock}"]},{"title":"Instructions","page":"Migrating from 2.x","href":"migration.html#instructions","text":"SPL 2.x SPL 3.0 --- --- > Summarize @notes and save it to @summary An ask block with outputVar: summary , or -> @summary in an agent block \"Save to @name\", -> @name as a convention the agent followed -> @name is syntax: it declares the output, and an agent may save only declared outputs @validate[Invoice] save to @invoice ->[Invoice] @invoice in an agent block; outputType: Invoice in an ask block typedef('Reviews', list(Review)) , then @validate[Reviews] The type is an expression: ->[List(Review)] @reviews @CTL(model='gpt-6-luna', reasoning='high') model: gpt-6-luna and reasoning: high @CTL(restrict={fs: ['./src'], exec: ['git']}) restrict: { fs: ./src, exec: git } @CTL(enforce='Be concise', forbid='guessing') enforce: Be concise and forbid: guessing @success and @fail in a blockquote success() and fail() in code, after the block has saved what they need The agent tools success , fail and restrict Removed; use code and the restrict header key @CODEGEN 'Define double(value)' A codegen block: Double a number -> @double(value: number): number @env.NAME , @args Unchanged @readFile : a library function as a reference Not needed: the exact tools are always available to an agent","group":"Guides","signatures":["> Summarize @notes and save it to @summary","-> @name","@validate[Invoice] save to @invoice","typedef('Reviews', list(Review))","@validate[Reviews]","@CTL(model='gpt-6-luna', reasoning='high')","@CTL(restrict={fs: ['./src'], exec: ['git']})","@CTL(enforce='Be concise', forbid='guessing')","@success","@fail","success","fail","restrict","@CODEGEN 'Define double(value)'","@env.NAME","@args","@readFile"]},{"title":"Control flow","page":"Migrating from 2.x","href":"migration.html#control-flow","text":"SPL 2.x SPL 3.0 --- --- if condition: , elif condition: , else: with indented blocks if (condition) { ... } else if (condition) { ... } else { ... } with blocks inside while condition: with indented blocks while (condition) { ... } {js alias=\"check\"} and go('check') A loop or a function: do { ... } while (...) , for , break , continue A condition described in the instruction if in code around the block Blocks are statements in 3.0, so TypeScript control flow replaces control lines and go(). A retry that jumped back with go(): becomes a loop:","group":"Guides","signatures":["if condition:","elif condition:","else:","while condition:","{js alias=\"check\"}","go('check')"]},{"title":"Library","page":"Migrating from 2.x","href":"migration.html#library","text":"SPL 2.x SPL 3.0 --- --- think(prompt, { reasoning: 'high' }) ask(prompt, { reasoning: 'high' }) reasoning : low , medium , high none , low , medium , high , max go(alias) Removed str , int , float , bool , char , nil , any , fn Str , Int , Float , Bool , Char , Nil , Any , Fn object Dict list(T) , union(A, B) , nullable(T) List(T) , Union(A, B) , Nullable(T) enumOf('a', 'b') Enum('a', 'b') struct(...) , typedef(...) , jsonSchema(...) Struct(...) , Typedef(...) , JsonSchema(...) t.str , t.enum , t.null , t.schema t.Str , t.Enum , t.Nil , t.JsonSchema Messages such as expected int, got \"x\" expected Int, got \"x\" No equivalent Type.coerce(value) The option top_p as an alias of topP topP only Agent tools read_file , write_file , web_fetch , web_search readFile , writeFile , webFetch , webSearch , also in logs and traces load_variable , save_variable , call_function , get_env , ask_human , set_guidance loadVariable , saveVariable , callFunction , getEnv , askHuman , setGuidance save_variable(name, value, schema) saveVariable(name, value) : the type comes from the output's declaration MCP tools numbered mcp_0_0 , mcp_0_1 , ... Named after the tool: mcp\u003cIndex>\u003cTool> , such as mcp0Search human, enforce, forbid, restrict, success, fail, assert, log, codegen, the exact tools, args, env, markdown, validate and Failure keep their names and behavior.","group":"Guides","signatures":["think(prompt, { reasoning: 'high' })","reasoning","low","medium","high","go(alias)","str","int","float","bool","char","nil","any","fn","object","list(T)","union(A, B)","nullable(T)","enumOf('a', 'b')","struct(...)","typedef(...)","jsonSchema(...)","t.str","t.enum","t.null","t.schema","expected int, got \"x\"","top_p","topP","read_file","write_file","web_fetch","web_search","load_variable","save_variable","call_function","get_env","ask_human","set_guidance","save_variable(name, value, schema)","mcp_0_0","mcp_0_1"]},{"title":"Behavior differences","page":"Migrating from 2.x","href":"migration.html#behavior-differences","text":"","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Embedding API","page":"Migrating from 2.x","href":"migration.html#embedding-api","text":"SPL 2.x SPL 3.0 --- --- parseMarkdown(text) or parseSpl(text) , then run(workflow, options) run(source, options) parse(code) , parseInstruction(text) , compileCell compile(source) , scan(source) checkWorkflow(workflow, cwd) check(file) ExecutionSession Removed Pin and the pin option Removed; use Trace The run options store , source , sourceVersion Removed; file names the workflow The run option beforeBlock beforeStep(step) CopilotBackend AiBackend request.kind === 'think' , request.naturalLanguage request.kind is ask or agent ; an agent block's request has runtimeTools See Embedding.","group":"Guides","signatures":["parseMarkdown(text)","parseSpl(text)","run(workflow, options)","parse(code)","parseInstruction(text)","compileCell","checkWorkflow(workflow, cwd)","ExecutionSession","Pin","pin","run","store","source","sourceVersion","run","beforeBlock","CopilotBackend","request.kind === 'think'","request.naturalLanguage"]},{"title":"Editor","page":"Migrating from 2.x","href":"migration.html#editor","text":"The VS Code extension no longer treats Markdown files as workflows and no longer has notebooks. For .spl files it colors Markdown comments, block headers and instructions, reports TypeScript diagnostics, completes and shows hovers, completes @name inside blocks, and has Run and Debug commands that open a terminal. See Getting started.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Checklist","page":"Migrating from 2.x","href":"migration.html#checklist","text":"","group":"Guides"}];
